    "<a id=\"section3\"></a>\n",
    "# Section 3 : The Neuron\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3.1 What a Single Neuron Does\n",
    "\n",
    "A single neuron is the building block of a neural network, and it is **exactly the dot product operation\n",
    "from Week 2**, with two small additions:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Take the inputs and multiply each one by a weight.\n",
    "2. Sum all of those weighted inputs together — this is the dot product from Week 2.\n",
    "3. Add a **bias** term.\n",
    "4. Pass the result through an **activation function**.\n",
    "\n",
    "In one line:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{output} = \\text{activation}\\big(\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{x} + b\\big)\n",
    "\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\n",
    "\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \\dots + w_n x_n\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "A neural network is just **many of these neurons, connected in layers**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2fc59eb1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.2 Implementing a Single Neuron\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "def sigmoid(z):\n",
    "    # A common activation function - squashes any real number into the (0, 1) range\n",
    "    return 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))\n",
    "\n",
    "def neuron(inputs, weights, bias, activation=sigmoid):\n",
    "    # Step 1 & 2: weighted sum - this IS the dot product from Week 2\n",
    "    z = np.dot(inputs, weights) + bias\n",
    "    # Step 3: activation\n",
    "    return activation(z)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example: a neuron with 3 inputs\n",
    "x = np.array([0.5, -1.2, 3.0])      # inputs\n",
    "w = np.array([0.4, 0.7, -0.3])      # weights (learned parameters)\n",
    "b = 0.1                             # bias (also learned)\n",
    "\n",
    "output = neuron(x, w, b)\n",
    "print(f\"z (weighted sum + bias) = {np.dot(x, w) + b:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"neuron output (after activation) = {output:.4f}\")\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Syntax explained:**\n",
    "- `np.dot(inputs, weights)` — the dot product from Week 2: multiplies each input by its corresponding\n",
    "  weight and sums the results into a single number.\n",
    "- `+ bias` — a constant added on top, letting the neuron shift its output up or down independent of the\n",
    "  inputs (the same role an intercept plays in linear/logistic regression).\n",
    "- `sigmoid(z)` — one common activation function; it squashes the raw weighted sum into a bounded range\n",
    "  `(0, 1)`, which is why it's often used when the output should look like a probability. Other activation\n",
    "  functions (ReLU, tanh, etc.) shape this differently, but every one of them takes the same `z` as input.\n"
   ]
